---
import Lecture from '../../../layouts/Lecture.astro';
import CodePlayground from '../../../components/CodePlayground.astro';
import Callout from '../../../components/Callout.astro';
import Quiz from '../../../components/Quiz.astro';
---

<Lecture
  chapterSlug="03-loops"
  lessonSlug="02-fizzbuzz"
  hook="The famous interview screener — every Ch. 3 loop skill plus the order-of-branches trap from Ch. 2 in one exercise."
>
  <h2>The rules</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Divisible by <strong>both 3 and 5</strong> → <code>"FizzBuzz"</code></li>
    <li>Divisible by <strong>3</strong> (only) → <code>"Fizz"</code></li>
    <li>Divisible by <strong>5</strong> (only) → <code>"Buzz"</code></li>
    <li>Otherwise → <code>str(i)</code></li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The shape</h2>
  <CodePlayground
    autoRun
    code={`def fizzbuzz(n):
    result = []
    for i in range(1, n + 1):
        if i % 15 == 0:                  # MOST specific first
            result.append("FizzBuzz")
        elif i % 3 == 0:
            result.append("Fizz")
        elif i % 5 == 0:
            result.append("Buzz")
        else:
            result.append(str(i))        # don't forget str(...)
    return result

for line in fizzbuzz(15):
    print(line)`}
  />

  <Callout tone="pitfall" title="Order trap (same as Letter Grade)">
    <p>
      If you put <code>i % 3 == 0</code> before <code>i % 15 == 0</code>,
      then <code>15</code> matches the divisible-by-3 branch first and
      returns <code>"Fizz"</code>. From Think Python §5.5: <em>"only the
      first true branch executes."</em>
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <Quiz
    question="In the BROKEN ordering (3-branch first), what does fizzbuzz(15)[14] return?"
    choices={[
      { text: '"FizzBuzz"', explain: "If the order were right — but with 3-first, 15 is caught by the divisible-by-3 branch and returns 'Fizz'." },
      { text: '"Fizz"', correct: true, explain: "Right. 15 % 3 == 0 fires first; the elif i % 15 == 0 branch is never reached." },
      { text: '"Buzz"', explain: "Buzz fires for multiples of 5 that aren't multiples of 3 — but with bad ordering, the 3-branch eats this case first too." },
      { text: '"15"', explain: "The else branch only fires when none of the earlier branches matched." },
    ]}
  />

  <h2>Why <code>str(i)</code> not just <code>i</code>?</h2>
  <CodePlayground
    caption="The TypeError-on-join trap"
    code={`def fizzbuzz_TYPE_BUG(n):
    result = []
    for i in range(1, n + 1):
        if i % 15 == 0:
            result.append("FizzBuzz")
        elif i % 3 == 0:
            result.append("Fizz")
        elif i % 5 == 0:
            result.append("Buzz")
        else:
            result.append(i)        # ✗ int, not str
    return result

out = fizzbuzz_TYPE_BUG(5)
try:
    print(", ".join(out))
except TypeError as e:
    print("Error:", e)`}
  />

  <h2>Now you try</h2>
  <p>
    Open <code>02-fizzbuzz.py</code>. The autograder runs 6 tests including
    a type-consistency check.
  </p>
</Lecture>
